  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Programmed withdrawal of cilia maintenance followed by centriole capping leads to permanent cilia loss during cerebellar granule cell neurogenesis (2023) in bioRxiv (Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory). 5 citations.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Programmed withdrawal of cilia maintenance followed by centriole capping leads to permanent cilia loss during cerebellar granule cell neurogenesis (2023) in bioRxiv (Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory) is authored by Sandii Constable, Carolyn M. Ott and Andrew L. Lemire. Topics: Cilium, Centriole, Cell biology, Neurogenesis and Intraflagellar transport.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">5 citations (OpenAlex). Citing venues include bioRxiv (Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory), Trends in Neurosciences and Current Biology.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Limited online visibility beyond citations.</p>",
  "provenance": {},
  "story_type": "ArticleStory",
  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This preprint has already attracted an early scholarly audience, with 5 citations in OpenAlex and 7 documented downstream reuse events. The record also shows that the work was later published in a peer-reviewed journal, suggesting that its cilia-deconstruction findings have begun to circulate beyond the preprint stage.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published as a bioRxiv preprint in 2023, this study examines how cerebellar granule cell neurogenesis culminates in the permanent loss of primary cilia. The paper focuses on a mechanism in which cilia maintenance is withdrawn and the centriole is capped, framing cilia disassembly as a programmed developmental process rather than a passive loss. Its conceptual center is clear: cilium biology, centriole dynamics, neurogenesis, and Sonic hedgehog signaling in the developing brain.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The article\u2019s impact profile is still early, but it is already visible in the citation record. OpenAlex reports 5 citations, all in 2024, and the citing literature spans a mix of articles, preprints, and a review. That pattern suggests the work is being taken up not just as a standalone result, but as part of a broader conversation about cilia dynamics across cell types and developmental contexts.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The reuse record reinforces that interpretation. There are 7 documented downstream reuse events, including scholarly articles, preprints, a review, and Europe PMC article mentions. Among the citing works are a peer-reviewed Journal of Cell Biology article with closely aligned authorship and topic, a Trends in Neurosciences review on neuronal cilia, and additional studies on cilia structure and stability in other systems. Together, these signals indicate that the study is helping shape an emerging research agenda around cilia maintenance and disassembly.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The dissemination trail also shows some stewardship beyond the article itself: one dataset citation is recorded, and the work is indexed in OpenAIRE. Public attention metrics are quiet in the available archive, so the strongest evidence here is scholarly reuse rather than broad public discussion. In that setting, the article reads as an influential early contribution to a fast-moving cell biology and neurodevelopmental literature.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
